abla E:\overline{
abla v}-k^2E\cdot\overline v)\,dx+\int_{\partial O}\kappa\,E_n\overline{v_n}\,dS-\langle T_RE,vangle_{S_R}$, where $
abla E:\overline{
abla v}:=\sum_{i,m}\partial_iE_m\overline{\partial_iv_m}$ and $\langle T_RE,vangle_{S_R}:=\sum_{m=1}^3\langle T_RE_m,v_mangle$ uses the scalar DtN pairing of [L1] on the components. Then $|a(E,v)|\le C\|E\|_{H^1(A)}\|v\|_{H^1(A)}$: the first two terms are bounded by Cauchy-Schwarz [L19]; the boundary term is bounded by $\|\kappa\|_\infty\|E\|_{L^2(\partial O)}\|v\|_{L^2(\partial O)}\le C'\|E\|_{H^1}\|v\|_{H^1}$ by [L10]; and the last by the boundedness of $T_R:H^{1/2}(S_R)	o H^{-1/2}(S_R)$ [L1] combined with the exact fractional trace bound [L21] for $H^1(A)$ functions on the smooth sphere $S_R$.

**The kernel of the form is trivial.** Let $E\in X$ satisfy $a(E,v)=0$ for every $v\in X$. Taking $v=E$ and imaginary parts in step 2.1 gives $0=\operatorname{Im}a(E,E)=-\operatorname{Im}\langle T_RE,Eangle$, where $f=E|_{S_R}$; by [L1] the imaginary pairing equals $\sum_m\sum_{l,m'}(\operatorname{Im}t_l)|(f_m)_{lm'}|^2$ with $\operatorname{Im}t_l>0$, so every coefficient vanishes, $f=0$ on $S_R$, and consequently $T_Rf=0$. For every $v\in X$ supported away from $\partial O$ the identity $a(E,v)=0$ has no inner-boundary contribution and reads $\int_A(
abla E:\overline{
abla v}-k^2E\cdot\overline v)\,dx=\langle T_RE,vangle_{S_R}=\langle T_Rf,v|_{S_R}angle=0$; in particular every $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(D)$, whose restriction to $A$ is an admissible test vanishing near $\partial O$, satisfies $\int_A
abla E:\overline{
abla\varphi}\,dx=k^2\int_AE\cdot\overline\varphi\,dx$, and taking $v$ compactly supported in the interior of $A$ gives $-\Delta E=k^2E$ weakly on $A$. Define $\widetilde E:=E$ on $A$ and $\widetilde E:=0$ on $\{|x|>R\}$; assign any value on the null sphere $S_R$, so $\widetilde E$ defines an $L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$ class, and its only interface is the sphere $S_R$, across which the full trace of the field is $f=0$. For $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(D)$ the Sobolev-Gauss-Green formula [L11] on the bounded $C^1$ domain $A$, applied componentwise with $u=E_m\in W^{1,2}(A)$ and $v=\varphi|_A$, gives $\int_AE_m\overline{\partial_i\varphi}\,dx=-\int_A(\partial_iE_m)\overline\varphi\,dx+\int_{\partial A}(TE_m)(T\varphi)
u_i\,dS$; the boundary term over $\partial O$ vanishes because $\varphi$ is compactly supported in $D$, and the term over $S_R$ vanishes because $TE_m=f_m=0$ there; hence $\int_D\widetilde E_m\overline{\partial_i\varphi}\,dx=-\int_A(\partial_iE_m)\overline\varphi\,dx$, so the distributional gradient of $\widetilde E$ is the field $(
abla E)\mathbf 1_A\in L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$, $\widetilde E\in H^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$, and the weak identity above gives $\int_D
abla\widetilde E:\overline{
abla\varphi}\,dx=k^2\int_D\widetilde E\cdot\overline\varphi\,dx$. By [L6] each real and imaginary part of each component of $\widetilde E$ lies in $H^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$ and satisfies $\Delta w=-k^2w$ distributionally; $D$ is nonempty, open and connected and $\widetilde E=0$ on the nonempty open set $D\cap\{|x|>R\}$, so [L5] gives $\widetilde E=0$ on $D$, in particular $E=0$ on $A$.
